the telemetry being connected

00:32:58.710 --> 00:33:03.060
to it through Dynatrace and
also through the Azure tools,

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:07.470
it started doing that
analysis in a sequential way.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:12.450
So, first, it looks at the
logs to understand which app

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:15.510
and then the revision
is causing the problem.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:19.410
And then it starts to look at
activity logs to figure out.

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:25.350
So, it found that just
now, three minutes ago,

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:28.380
the password got
rotated, which is true.

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:31.380
So, I think it's
getting to the sort

00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:35.160
of correlation of what happened.

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:37.980
And is it like
matching the symptoms

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:40.330
that the user is experiencing?

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:48.210
So, bingo.

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:51.870
It figured out that the
database password is rotated,

00:33:51.870 --> 00:33:54.660
but the container app
hasn't been updated

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:58.930
and that's why it's actually
causing the failure.

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:19.320
So, now it sort of
confirmed the root cause

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:23.340
because it also has access to
the issues that the agent saw

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:26.820
from the past as well,
that this is a recurring,

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:28.560
credential desync pattern.

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:32.250
So, now it's going to create a
new issue in the GitHub to track

00:34:32.250 --> 00:34:35.310
and also to figure out
what's a remediation.

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:39.210
So, as you can see, the SRE
Agent is not just trying

00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:43.380
to diagnose the issue based on
what it saw, but it's also like,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:45.900
you know, how as an
expert engineer would tap

00:34:45.900 --> 00:34:49.380
into their past knowledge
or past incidents

00:34:49.380 --> 00:34:51.930
that they have seen
in the past to figure

00:34:51.930 --> 00:34:54.270
out whether there is
some sort of a pattern

00:34:54.270 --> 00:34:57.510
or a similar analysis
that can be applied here.

00:34:57.510 --> 00:35:00.720
It's doing something
similar as well.

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:03.030
And it's using the
data to validate

00:35:03.030 --> 00:35:06.570
and support its hypothesis,
and the assumptions

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:09.650
as it's making throughout
the investigation.

00:35:09.650 --> 00:35:14.750
And this is just happening, you
know, within minutes which means

00:35:14.750 --> 00:35:16.910
in reality, figuring
these things

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:21.600
out would have taken
multiple hours and switching

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:23.940
between different
tools and contexts.

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:27.770
But the agent is just acting
on that and you don't have

00:35:27.770 --> 00:35:31.670
to actually look at the agent
as it's finishing the analysis

00:35:31.670 --> 00:35:35.120
because the incident is
picked up automatically

00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:41.220
and you can just look at your
GitHub issues or the tools

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.290
through which you can
receive the information

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:46.770
like even connected to
an Outlook or teams

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:50.220
and just get the message
out, without having to wait

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:53.070
for the analysis to be done.

00:35:53.070 --> 00:35:58.860
I actually configured the agent
such that it can perform actions

00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:02.580
on the resources or perform
mitigations autonomously.

00:36:02.580 --> 00:36:06.150
And in this case, that is why it
proceeded with the remediation

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:09.600
as well, because it also felt
like it's a recurring pattern

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:12.630
and in order to fix the issue
right away we need to sort

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:16.200
of update the password
with the new credentials

00:36:16.200 --> 00:36:19.920
and make sure it sort of flows
down to the apps as well.

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:23.200
So, it went ahead and
did the remediation.

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:27.930
But in theory, you can
configure the agent

00:36:27.930 --> 00:36:30.420
such that it gives
you the analysis

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:35.230
and does not do any automated
actions on your behalf.

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:56.580
So, you see the final summary,
it figured out the sequence

00:36:56.580 --> 00:36:58.530
of the things that
led to the root cause

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:02.580
of the password being changed,
and it went ahead and fixed it.

00:37:02.580 --> 00:37:06.060
And it also checked to make sure
that the new revision is healthy

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.620
and it created a GitHub issue.

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:10.170
There you go.

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:14.220
You went from your incidents
being manually handled

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:17.070
through like the agent
actually automating it

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:22.530
and providing a mitigation, plus
the summary of all the issue

00:37:22.530 --> 00:37:27.060
in a trackable GitHub issue.

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:30.180
VYOM NAGRANI: Perfect,
thank you so much, Deepthi.

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:33.210
So, now let's move to a
very important question.

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:36.540
Let's talk about how we've
built trust and control

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:40.560
into every layer of the Azure
SRE Agent across things

00:37:40.560 --> 00:37:43.560
like permissions, governance,
security and monitoring.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:45.990
So, let's start
with permissions.

00:37:45.990 --> 00:37:49.020
There are three built-in
user roles like a reader,

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:51.800
a standard user and
an administrator.

00:37:51.800 --> 00:37:53.000
Each of these are scoped

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:55.640
to exactly what the
persona that's interacting

00:37:55.640 --> 00:37:57.350
with the agent would need.

00:37:57.350 --> 00:38:00.470
So, when you create your agent,
you can choose between a reader

00:38:00.470 --> 00:38:02.300
or privilege permission level

00:38:02.300 --> 00:38:05.460
for the agent's
managed identity.

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:08.910
Now, every agent gets its own
user assigned managed identity,

00:38:08.910 --> 00:38:13.170
so there are no secrets
or API keys to manage.

00:38:13.170 --> 00:38:14.070
And when the agent needs

00:38:14.070 --> 00:38:17.850
to perform an action beyond
its standing permission,

00:38:17.850 --> 00:38:21.750
it uses the "on behalf
of" or the "OBO" flow

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:25.650
to temporary elevate
using your credentials.

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:29.370
So, no standing right
access required.

00:38:29.370 --> 00:38:32.490
On the governance part, you
control how the agent behaves

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:34.140
through different run modes.

00:38:34.140 --> 00:38:37.140
The review mode means that
the agent proposes the action

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:39.390
and waits for an
administrator to approve,

00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:42.200
while the autonomous mode
lets it execute directly.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:46.400
That's ideal for non-product
trusted recurring tasks.

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:51.140
We also support command
validation hooks that inspect

00:38:51.140 --> 00:38:54.600
and validate every
command before execution

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:57.810
or every tool call
before it's returned.

00:38:57.810 --> 00:39:01.440
And every single action that the
agent takes, from tool calls

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:04.440
to modeling locations
to approval decisions,

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:06.690
all of this is logged
to Application Insights

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:11.850
as a full audit trail that
you can query with KQL.

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:13.170
On the security front,

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.690
the architecture is
secret, less by design.

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:19.560
Credentials are never entering
the reasoning context

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:22.230
and an isolated
identity sidecar issued

00:39:22.230 --> 00:39:26.250
with short-lived per token calls
is where the permissions live.

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:30.600
Each agent run its own dedicated
sandbox powered by Micro VM,

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:34.260
which is an Azure dedicated
compute environment as are wrote

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:38.460
to your Python tools, so no
cross agent data is leaked.

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:41.760
Connectors to external
systems like GitHub, Datadog,

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:45.890
ServiceNow all use OAuth
based authentication flows,

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:49.010
and all outbound network access
goes through an egress proxy

00:39:49.010 --> 00:39:50.540
that validates the requests,

00:39:50.540 --> 00:39:52.610
controls what each
agent can reach

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:55.700
or you can bring your own VNETs.

00:39:55.700 --> 00:39:57.920
And finally, for monitoring,

00:39:57.920 --> 00:40:00.080
Session Insights gives
you a structured summary

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:02.540
of any conversation,
what was found,

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:05.130
what was recommended, and so on.

00:40:05.130 --> 00:40:08.640
The incident metrics dashboard
shows mitigation rates,

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:12.990
hours saved, the
per response plan,

00:40:12.990 --> 00:40:15.570
as well as at a summary level.

00:40:15.570 --> 00:40:19.260
And the agent consumption lets
you track exactly how many Azure

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:23.040
AI units the agent was used
on a day-to-day basis.

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:26.940
We have inbuilt evals like
the Intent Met quality score

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:28.950
which are automatically
calculated

00:40:28.950 --> 00:40:32.190
on every completed thread and
rated on a one to five scale,

00:40:32.190 --> 00:40:34.860
so you can measure
effectiveness over time.

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:39.090
Bottom line across all of these,
you get full autonomous power

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:43.800
of things like Azure SRE Agent
with full enterprise controls.

00:40:43.800 --> 00:40:44.970
Now, there are three ways

00:40:44.970 --> 00:40:47.040
in which you can use
the Azure SRE agent,

00:40:47.040 --> 00:40:48.120
and these are designed to work

00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:50.300
across your full
operational lifecycle.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:52.490
The first is the
interactive mode.

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:54.950
You can chat directly with the
agent in the Azure portal,

00:40:54.950 --> 00:40:57.020
ask it questions
and diagnostics,

00:40:57.020 --> 00:40:59.420
perform natural
language operations,

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:05.010
upload your task files, HAR
files for traces or analysis.

00:41:05.010 --> 00:41:08.370
Think of this as like the
Copilot mode, which assumes

00:41:08.370 --> 00:41:10.890
that a human is
driving the flow.

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:13.200
But if this is the only
thing you use the agent for,

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:15.600
you're probably missing
90% of the value

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:19.560
that an agent can unlock
beyond a Copilot.

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:22.860
The primary way most customers
use the SRE Agent is

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:24.660
as a reactive mode.

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:27.270
This is the alert
driven response mode.

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.360
The agent responds automatically
to Azure Monitor alerts

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:34.110
and integrates with PagerDuty
and ServiceNow as well.

00:41:34.110 --> 00:41:37.410
It performs automated triage,
initial root cause analysis

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:39.870
and then can even trigger the
mitigation with your approval,

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:42.330
as we saw in the demo.

00:41:42.330 --> 00:41:46.020
It tracks incident metrics and
insights over time for this.

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:48.240
And the last one is
the proactive mode.

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:51.030
So, this is where the agent
operates autonomously

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:52.340
on a schedule.

00:41:52.340 --> 00:41:54.350
It runs things like
cron expressions

00:41:54.350 --> 00:41:57.740
where you can perform continuous
health and compliance checks.

00:41:57.740 --> 00:42:01.370
You can try to detect anomalies
across different resources.

00:42:01.370 --> 00:42:04.100
You can assess the security
posture of your stack,

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:07.190
and it can provide resource
optimization recommendations.

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:11.090
So, across these three modes,
they give you a full coverage

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:14.840
from ad hoc troubleshooting to
always on autonomous operation

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:16.860
that runs in the background.

00:42:16.860 --> 00:42:19.110
So, then why should you
choose the Azure SRE Agent?

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:20.790
So, it's built
natively on Azure,

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:23.580
which means it understands
your infrastructure deeply,

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:24.570
your resource graph,

00:42:24.570 --> 00:42:27.240
your monitor signals,
your entity model.

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:30.390
It's extensible, so it adapts
to your environment rather

00:42:30.390 --> 00:42:33.290
than forcing you into
a rigid workflow.

00:42:33.290 --> 00:42:35.930
It's secured by design
with managed identity,

00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:38.210
RBAC, full audit trims.

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:40.550
It integrates with the tools
that you're already using

00:42:40.550 --> 00:42:43.610
across GitHub, PagerDuty
as ServiceNow, Datadog,

00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:47.910
Dynatrace whatever, that
you're using in your systems.

00:42:47.910 --> 00:42:50.550
And whether you're a
team of a single SRE,

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:54.450
a team of five operations
managers, or an organization

00:42:54.450 --> 00:42:56.430
that supports
thousands of services,

00:42:56.430 --> 00:42:58.830
Azure SRE Agent meets
you where you are

00:42:58.830 --> 00:43:02.050
and it scales to
your requirements.

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.520
So, let's leave you with
how to quickly get started.

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:07.260
It's a simple three
step process.

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:09.030
Step one, you teach it.

00:43:09.030 --> 00:43:11.640
You upload your runbooks,
your documentation,

00:43:11.640 --> 00:43:16.130
connect your code repositories,
share the team knowledge

00:43:16.130 --> 00:43:17.810
and the agent learns
from every session,

00:43:17.810 --> 00:43:20.490
just like you would
with a human SRE.

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:22.470
Step two, it connects.

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:25.980
The agent integrates with Azure
Monitor, Application Insights,

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:29.850
PagerDuty, ServiceNow,
Datadog, all of these systems,

00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:33.210
basically anything which can
connect it through an MCP.

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:34.830
Step three, it works for you.

00:43:34.830 --> 00:43:37.170
It investigates the
incidents around the clock,

00:43:37.170 --> 00:43:39.180
it runs scheduled
compliance scans,

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:42.360
triggers issues automatically,
proposes fixes.

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:45.060
You just give it context, it
connects to your tool and it --

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:46.410
it starts working for you.

00:43:46.410 --> 00:43:49.980
Imagine like a teammate
that never sleeps.

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:51.240
So, that's all we
had for you today.

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:53.580
Thank you all for
attending today's session.

